  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 442 AM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThings will finally feel much more Fall-like to open the long term \nforecast period Friday night into Saturday morning as a sharp cold \nfront will rapidly progress across the CWA. Gusty NW winds (likely \ngusts near/over 30 mph at times) will transition toward the north \nand northeast on Saturday as surface high pressure slips across the \nMid-Atlantic. This frontal passage continues to look very dry with \nany isolated sprinkles/brief showers largely relegated to north \nGeorgia, though these will be very limited in nature.\n\nBy Saturday morning, temperatures will have fallen into the 40s \nacross a good bit of north Georgia, a drop of nearly 40 degrees in \nsome areas from Friday highs. Expect below-normal temperatures to \npersist through the weekend with highs mainly in the 60s each day \nand lows in the 40s for most (some 30s in the mountains) through \nMonday. A warming trend will then be underway by next week as \nmidlevel ridging builds eastward with temperatures near to slightly \nabove normal by midweek. Still, no notable rain chances are in the \nforeseeable future as surface high pressure will continue to build \nsouthwestward from New England through the week.",
